# Decoded ACPI-WMI Binary MOF for Lenovo 82JU (Legion 5 15ACH6H)

## TL;DR
- **The blobs are NOT encrypted or obfuscated.** They are Microsoft **DS-01
  (DoubleSpace) compressed Binary-MOF (BMF/BMOF)**. Class names are invisible to
  `strings` only because the payload is LZ77-compressed.
- `46 4F 4D 42` = `'F' 'O' 'M' 'B'` = the BMOF signature `0x424D4F46` stored
  little-endian. Immediately after the 16-byte header the stream begins `44 53`
  = DS-01 signature `0x5344` ('DS', byte-swapped) + version `00 01`.
- Windows reads these through the `wmi-bmof` object (GUID
  `05901221-D566-11D1-B2F0-00A0C9062910`, ObjectId `44 41`/`30 30`/`44 44`/
  `42 41`/`42 44` depending on device) which returns the **compressed** buffer.
  Reference decoder: https://github.com/pali/bmfdec (`bmfdec.c` = DS-01,
  `bmfparse.c` = BMOF). Pure-python port: `80-tools/bmof2mof.py`.

## 1. Byte-level container evidence
Header, 4x u32 little-endian:

| off  | field |
|------|-------|
| 0x00 | `46 4F 4D 42` signature `0x424D4F46` |
| 0x04 | version `0x00000001` |
| 0x08 | compressed size (DS-01 stream length) |
| 0x0C | decompressed size (BMF payload length) |

Total buffer length = 16 + compressed size.  All five buffers found by scanning
`20-acpi/aml/*.aml`:

| AML Name | src | device (_UID) | first 0x10 bytes | comp | decomp |
|----------|-----|---------------|------------------|------|--------|
| `WQBD` | DSDT.dsl:15099 | `WMI4` (0x04) | `46 4F 4D 42 01 00 00 00 65 02 00 00 F8 05 00 00` | 0x0265=613 | 0x05F8=1528 |
| `WQDD` | DSDT.dsl:22186 | `GZFD` ("GMZN") | `46 4F 4D 42 01 00 00 00 5D 4D 00 00 C0 FC 01 00` | 0x4D5D=19805 | 0x0001FCC0=130240 |
| `WQDA` | DSDT.dsl:24944 | `WMIU` ("LSK20") | `46 4F 4D 42 01 00 00 00 08 04 00 00 5E 0C 00 00` | 0x0408=1032 | 0x0C5E=3166 |
| `WQBA` | SSDT3.dsl:2690 | `AOD` ("AOD") | `46 4F 4D 42 01 00 00 00 47 0A 00 00 38 37 00 00` | 0x0A47=2631 | 0x3738=14136 |
| `WQ00` | SSDT6.dsl:4296 | `AWMI` ("0x00") | `46 4F 4D 42 01 00 00 00 6D 03 00 00 68 0B 00 00` | 0x036D=877 | 0x0B68=2920 |

Every DS-01 stream starts `44 53 00 01 1A 7D DA 54 ...`; `WQBD` and `WQDA`
further share `... 42 10 05 10 8A 42 04/28 81 ...` i.e. an identical
decompressed prefix (fixed BMOF header + shared UTF-16 strings). Identical
plaintext prefixes survive a lossless transform but never a cipher -- direct
proof this is compression, not encryption. (Sizes also satisfy the bmfdec
`comp == filesize-16` assertion for all five.)

## 2. Decompressed payload (BMOF) layout
Recovered from pali/bmfdec:
- `'FOMB'` (0x424D4F46) + u32 `len(first_part)`; part 1 at 0x08.
- If more data follows: at absolute offset `len`, ASCII `BMOFQUALFLAVOR11`
  (16 B), u32 `count`, then `count` entries of 8 B = (absolute qualifier offset,
  flavor bits). Bits: 0x01 ToInstance, 0x02 ToSubclass, 0x10 DisableOverride,
  0x80 Amended.
- Part 1 = root: u32 1, u32 1, u32 nclasses, then nclasses length-prefixed
  class records.
- Class = u32, u32 0, u32 qual_len, u32 data_len, u32 instance_flag(0),
  class qualifiers, data, method table. Data = u32 size1, u32 nquals, quals,
  u32 len2, u32 nvars, variables, then trailing `__CLASS`/`__NAMESPACE`/
  `__SUPERCLASS`/`__CLASSFLAGS` properties. Item lengths are u32 prefixes;
  strings are UTF-16LE. Class->GUID comes from the class `guid("{...}")`
  string qualifier.
- Basic type codes: `02`=SInt16 `03`=SInt32 `04`=Real32 `05`=Real64 `08`=String
  `0B`=Boolean `10`=SInt8 `11`=UInt8 `12`=UInt16 `13`=UInt32 `14`=SInt64
  `15`=UInt64 `65`=Datetime `67`=Char16 `0D`=object; `type>>8==0x20` => array;
  `CIMTYPE`/`MAX` qualifiers carry the effective type and array bound.

